     aatt takes a list of commands and arranges for them to be ex-
     ecuted at the given _t_i_m_e. When the commands run, the user
     id, group id, exported shell variables, and current direc-
     tory are all as they were when aatt was invoked.

     _t_i_m_e is one to four digits giving hours and optionally
     minutes, followed optionally by aa, pp, nn, or mm for A.M.,
     P.M., noon, or midnight.  _t_i_m_e may be followed by a _d_a_y of
     the week, optionally followed by the word wweeeekk to mean the
     following week, or by a _m_o_n_t_h name and a numeric day of the
     month.  If no day or month is given, the command runs at the
     next occurrence of _t_i_m_e today or tomorrow.

     aatt recognizes the following options:

     -cc _c_o_m_m_a_n_d
          Take the commands to run from the string _c_o_m_m_a_n_d rather
          than from standard input or _f_i_l_e.

     -vv   Print the date and time at which the command will be
          executed.

     If a _f_i_l_e argument is given, aatt takes the commands to run
     from it.  If neither _f_i_l_e nor -cc is given, aatt reads the com-
     mands from the standard input.

     aatt copies the commands, the current environment, umask, and
     working directory into a shell script in directory
     /uussrr/ssppooooll/aatt, named for the year, month, day, hour, and
     minute at which it is to run, and sets the set-user-id, set-
     group-id, and owner-execute bits on that file.  The command
     aattrruunn watches this directory and, when the time arrives,
     runs the script with the user and group id recorded in its
